# Presentation of the 1967-68 Budget Estimates
Tamil title: 1967-68 ஆம் ஆண்டுக்குரிய வரவு-செலவுத் திட்டங்களைப் பேரவை முன் வைத்தல்

Speech delivered by Tamil Nadu Chief Minister C. N. Annadurai before the Tamil Nadu Legislative Assembly on 20 March 1967.

Mr Speaker,

I regard it as a great privilege to have the opportunity to place my first budget before the new Legislative Assembly. The budget for 1967-68 now placed before the House is an interim budget. Nevertheless, in accordance with the provisions of the Constitution, the estimates have been prepared for the whole year.

This budget has been framed in keeping with the commitments and policies already accepted by the Government, so that existing development works and the ordinary business of government may continue without interruption while the new Ministry undertakes a detailed review of those policies and programmes. The 1967-68 estimates make no provision for what are called “Part II schemes” or “new schemes” that the various departments wish to undertake during the coming year. The present Government must first consider those schemes and provide for them according to the resources available.

The 1967-68 estimates will therefore be reconsidered and revised to provide for new schemes and policies, and the Government will place the revised budget before the Assembly around June 1967. Although the present estimates cover the whole year, demands for the full grants included in them will not be placed before the Assembly during this session. After the general discussion on the estimates, a Vote on Account will be sought to meet expenditure expected during the first four months of the next financial year.

A White Paper briefly explaining the final accounts for 1965-66, the revised estimates for 1966-67, and the budget estimates now prepared for 1967-68 has been placed before the House along with the other budget documents. I shall therefore avoid discussing them at length and draw your attention only to certain major features.

The revised estimates for the current year are based on figures submitted by the various government departments between October and December 1966. They now indicate a revenue-account deficit of Rs. 245 lakh instead of the small surplus of Rs. 31 lakh originally expected. Despite this large revenue deficit, the State's overall financial position, taking together the revenue, debt, capital, and public accounts, is expected to balance, with approximately the closing cash balance anticipated in the original estimates.

Several decisions and developments after the budget was passed account for this deterioration in the revenue account. I shall mention the most important briefly. Following increases in dearness allowance for Central Government employees, the State had to grant two corresponding increases to its own employees, including employees of local authorities and teachers in aided schools. The first increase, effective from 1 January 1966, costs Rs. 3.6 crore in a full year and Rs. 2.7 crore in the current year. The second, effective from 1 December 1966, costs Rs. 5.9 crore in a full year and about Rs. 1.5 crore in the current year alone.

The abolition from 1 April 1966 of the one per cent multi-point sales tax on foodgrains caused a revenue loss of about Rs. 1.5 crore. Pending a comprehensive decision on land revenue, removal of the 25 per cent Land Revenue Surcharge caused another loss of approximately Rs. 1.5 crore.

When preparing the 1966-67 budget, we expected the State's share of additional Central taxation to yield Rs. 3.5 crore. That was the figure indicated during discussions with the Planning Commission while settling the 1966-67 annual plan. The Central budget proposals included two measures intended to provide additional resources to the States: raising Central sales tax from two to three per cent, and revising the rates of Union Excise Duties shared with the States.

Present calculations show that Central sales tax will yield only about Rs. 1 crore in additional revenue, while our share from the higher Union Excise Duties will be only about Rs. 70 lakh. Thus, against the Rs. 3.5 crore originally anticipated, our total share of additional Central taxation will be only Rs. 1.70 crore.

I must also emphasise that, because economic growth has slowed, the rate of growth in State taxes and in the divisible Central taxes is considerably lower this year than during the early years of the Third Plan. These factors together have produced the substantial revenue deficit in the revised estimates.

As in previous years, final supplementary appropriations may involve some expenditure beyond the sums now provided in the revised estimates. We are trying to keep such additions to the minimum. Any unavoidable expenditure may be offset by growth in revenue, and we expect the State's final financial position to remain broadly unchanged. The final supplementary estimates I shall place before the House tomorrow will show the amount of expenditure beyond the revised estimates.

The year began satisfactorily with a cash balance of Rs. 735 lakh, including an outstanding Ways and Means Advance of Rs. 375 lakh, leaving a net balance of Rs. 360 lakh. Yet the ways and means position remained difficult. Increased capital expenditure and unfavourable trends in several capital receipts created pressure during the year.

Following the Chinese aggression, the State had increased provident-fund contribution rates for State employees, including employees of local authorities and aided-school teachers. In response to their representations, the Government abandoned that arrangement from June 1966. This reduced net receipts by about Rs. 1 crore below the original estimate for the current year.

While preparing the 1966-67 estimates, we expected to issue an Open Market Loan of Rs. 14 crore. Subsequent money-market conditions and the Reserve Bank's advice required the issue to be reduced to Rs. 13 crore. Nor did small-savings receipts improve beyond the original estimates as they had in earlier years. Despite these adverse trends, we expect the agreed Central assistance to be received and our accounts to close with a balance. The House will also welcome our firm expectation that the year can be completed without unauthorised overdrafts from the Reserve Bank.

It is against this background that next year's estimates must be examined. In estimating receipts, we have assumed reasonable growth in elastic State resources such as sales tax, motor-vehicles tax, and electricity-consumption tax.

The Government has not yet decided the future of land-revenue policy. The report of the Special Officer appointed to study every aspect of abolishing land revenue has only recently been received, and the Government requires time to consider it. For the present, the Finance Department has included normal land-revenue collections while excluding only the surcharge already abolished. The Government's decision on land revenue will therefore appear only in the regular budget to be placed before the Assembly.

On revenue expenditure, only minimal increases have been allowed under non-development heads. Even growth in Maintenance Expenditure under development heads has been kept low so that resources may be preserved for development works forming part of the Plan.

On this basis, revenue receipts for 1967-68 are estimated at Rs. 204.39 crore and expenditure at Rs. 211.99 crore, producing a revenue deficit of Rs. 760 lakh. This is slightly greater than the Rs. 745 lakh deficit expected in the current year and is principally due to the second increase in dearness allowance.

If the net receipts of the capital and public accounts could balance the budget as a whole, the revenue deficit alone would not cause grave concern. But the estimates also show a Negative Closing Cash Balance of Rs. 885 lakh. A major Open Market Loan matures next year, requiring repayment of Rs. 10.47 crore. After consultation with the Reserve Bank, we expect to raise a new loan of Rs. 18 crore. Once the maturing loan is repaid, only Rs. 7.53 crore will remain.

In the current year we raised Rs. 13.24 crore without any repayment obligation. The public-debt position next year will therefore be worse by Rs. 5.71 crore, and this alone is a major cause of the imbalance in the coming year's budget.

At this stage we leave both the revenue deficit and the overall deficit uncovered. Although the first year of the Fourth Plan is nearing completion, the Fourth Plan itself has not yet been finalised. The 1966-67 annual plan was prepared mainly to carry forward schemes left incomplete under the Third Plan, essential agricultural-production projects, and unavoidable increases in social services such as education, medical aid, and public health.

Following recent discussions with State Governments, the Planning Commission has provisionally approved our State's draft Fourth Five-Year Plan. The proposed State investment is Rs. 564 crore. The Central Government is to provide Rs. 250 crore, while the State must raise the remaining Rs. 314 crore.

After those discussions, the Planning Commission decided to transfer certain Centrally sponsored schemes, together with their financial resources, to the State sector. It has not yet calculated the additional sums each State will receive. Including these transfers, we may expect Tamil Nadu's Fourth Plan investment to be no less than Rs. 600 crore.

The Fourth Five-Year Plan has not yet been formally accepted by the National Development Council. The State Government will promptly review its draft and decide what essential adjustments in Priorities are required. As part of that review, it will reassess the resources available to execute the Plan. This will naturally take some time, so its conclusions will appear in the regular budget placed before the Assembly.

At the national level, uncertainty about Plan resources continues and will end only when the prospects of foreign assistance become clear. The Planning Commission has therefore asked the States to prepare only interim plans for 1967-68. These must provide for agricultural-production programmes, irrigation projects already supported by foreign aid, and power projects. States have been left to make whatever provision they can for other sectors, including social services.

The Central Government too will present only an interim budget to Parliament at existing tax rates. Only Rs. 35 crore is expected to be provided in it as Central assistance for our State Plan next year, substantially below the assistance accepted for the current year.

In these circumstances, we examined how far next year's Plan investment could be reduced without slowing or disrupting works already under execution. The revised investment in the current annual plan is about Rs. 80 crore. Even accepting a lower rate of growth and permitting only a few indispensable new schemes in social services and other sectors, next year's Plan would ordinarily need to be about Rs. 90 crore.

Despite present constraints, we consider that provision for Plan works in the interim budget cannot be less than Rs. 75.78 crore. Any further reduction would seriously affect major power, irrigation, and agricultural-production projects and delay returns from investments already made. Even this restricted interim Plan leaves a resource gap of Rs. 9 crore. If new schemes were included and cuts under minor irrigation, rural electrification, and other important heads restored, the gap would widen.

We have nevertheless framed a deficit Plan for two broad reasons. First, when the 1967-68 estimates are revised in light of the current year's final accounts, particularly if the economy improves in the meantime, our resource position may become somewhat better. Second, when the Central Government prepares its regular budget, Central assistance for the State Plan will necessarily have to be increased.

Of the total Central assistance of Rs. 250 crore agreed for five years, we may normally expect at least 18 to 19 per cent in the second year. Work on major projects such as the Ennore Thermal Power Project and the Parambikulam-Aliyar hydroelectric project began during the Third Plan, and the investment required under them will reach its peak next year. Slowing their completion would not serve the national interest.

Our provisions for minor irrigation and rural electrification are also much lower than current-year expenditure. They must be raised at least to the current level if agricultural-production targets are to be achieved. These facts strengthen our claim for additional Central assistance. We shall press that claim forcefully, and such assistance should cover a large part of the final deficit in the 1967-68 estimates.

We fully recognise the importance of a Balanced Budget in the present state of the economy. Under today's severe price conditions, deficit financing must not continue. But the State budget's present deficit arises partly from circumstances beyond the Government's control, directly from the large reduction in Central assistance for works already under execution.

Reducing assistance may relieve the Central budget and help it present a balance. It is nevertheless neither practical nor fair for the Centre to limit its deficit by cutting assistance to States and placing entirely upon them the difficult task of reshaping programmes to fit diminished resources. Avoiding deficit finance requires a major revision of schemes and priorities, including Central schemes and non-plan Central expenditure.

The Central Government and the Planning Commission must consider the country's total resources, including those of both Central and State Governments, and examine how they can be more fairly distributed between Central and State plans for maximum benefit. The urgent need is to increase agricultural production through projects yielding quick returns and relieve pressure on prices. Most agricultural-development programmes fall within State Plans; cutting them would be unwise.

While preparing the regular plan for 1967-68, I trust that the Planning Commission will assess priorities in both sectors impartially, taking account of present economic conditions, and provide States adequate resources for their most essential projects. We should not hesitate to postpone less important Central-sector projects if necessary.

The problem of State overdrafts from the Reserve Bank can be solved only by allocating States more resources on a realistic basis within the Plan framework. This issue has generated vigorous public debate. We do not minimise the need for States to manage their finances vigilantly or to do everything possible to mobilise resources for development. We shall cooperate fully with the Centre in every effort to cultivate prudent financial administration.

But when a State maintaining high financial discipline encounters temporary difficulty in carrying out approved schemes, the Central Government and Planning Commission should help it meet that difficulty. If overdrafts are treated merely as bookkeeping or creditor-debtor questions, without sympathy or political wisdom, State development will suffer severely.

Another major pressure upon limited State resources is the repeated need to revise dearness allowance for State employees. Such revisions are indispensable and wholly justified while prices continue to rise. The Fourth Finance Commission allowed for increases sanctioned up to 1965-66. Before its award took effect on 1 April 1966, however, the Centre granted another increase. It later accepted further increases recommended in the interim report of the Gajendragadkar Commission, and our State had to follow.

The combined effect of those two increases is reflected in both the revenue deficit and the overall deficit for next year. Additional resources arising from the Finance Commission's award were intended only to meet committed expenditure on establishments and services created during the previous Plan period. Its arrangements therefore leave most States with neither surplus nor deficit on the Non-Plan Account, but make no provision for new non-plan liabilities arising later.

The Fourth Finance Commission recognised this serious defect but considered it beyond its terms of reference and could offer no solution. Since its award was not final when the issue arose, we have again urged before the Gajendragadkar Commission the need to allot States adequate resources for the additional expenditure caused by dearness allowance. I hope its final report will provide a fair and lasting solution to this longstanding obstacle to sound State financial administration.

10. The present Government will act immediately to prepare the regular plan for 1967-68 and place it before the Assembly around June. By then, the amount of Central assistance available to the State Plan will be clear. I trust that we shall then be able to place before the House a plan that more effectively fulfils the aspirations and hopes arising in our people's hearts. With that confidence, I ask the House to accept the 1967-68 budget estimates.

---NOTES---
The speech is an interim budget statement. It repeatedly distinguishes the temporary estimates presented in March from the regular budget and revised State Plan intended for June 1967.

Amounts are retained in the Indian units printed in the source: one lakh is 100,000 rupees and one crore is 10 million rupees.

`Ways and Means Advance`, `Negative Closing Cash Balance`, `Non-Plan Account`, and the other English accounting labels reproduce the wording printed in the scans and restored during OCR reconciliation.

The Gajendragadkar Commission reference concerns the commission examining dearness allowance for government employees; the source spells the name in Tamil as `கஜேந்திரகாட்கர்`.
